{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of DX-A2 at head aa20f0fe0e01e380e49497a64371958e1147e574, tree 07baa6da, for request 1c124ebf and review request ee9fd48b. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED.\n\nThe head adds five commits to main 5dc9765f and changes 45 paths. I read the implementation note first, then the diff of every supporting path, against request 1c124ebf, ruling 261004b1, wire adoption dc7be8f4 and section 7 of `docs/kernel.md`. An independent read of the same source ran beside mine. I confirmed each finding below in the code. None was reproduced at runtime, and each fix should bring its own case.\n\nThe barrier's core is sound. Authority runs before any head and again at every wake, a seal cannot be missed, nothing waits inside a turn or a transaction, and the wire is closed and bounded. The defects are in what the barrier does to existing answers. A receipt below the journal floor loses its `archived` answer, one waiting read blocks other readers of the scope, a causal signed reader can miss the entry it waited for, and two public refusals change while the docs still promise them.\n\nI do not accept the single-file follow-up for gate 3, for the reason in item 4. Items 1 to 3 change production source in any case, so a new full gate must run at the final head.\n\n## Changes requested\n\n1. A full-head minimum below the floor loses the `archived` answer.\n   - `ReadWait.until` compares the minimum's hash with `hash(seq)` (`read-wait.ts:33`), which reads `store.stored(seq)`. A floor deletes that row, so the result is null and the read returns `unavailable`. `ScopeObject.entry` returns that refusal (`object.ts:944`) before `Reads.entry` reaches `archivedPosition`.\n   - Scenario: `followReceipt(receipt)` with a position below the floor. Main returns `archived` with the floor, commit and through position. This head returns `unavailable`, so the reader loses the archive pointer and sees what reads as a passing failure. The `archived` branch in `followReceipt` no longer runs against an honest server.\n   - The same applies to summary, items, history and projection when given such a receipt. Without a minimum they answer normally.\n   - A card keeps the failure. `ViewSessions` sets `readReceipt` on an accepted act (`session.ts:278`), never clears it, and sends it on each refresh (`session.ts:184`). Once the floor passes that position, every refresh fails until a new act or a new instance.\n   - The note and adoption dc7be8f4 state \"missing retained proof uses unavailable\", and no test covers a floor. A floor is an ordinary state, not missing proof.\n   - Fix: a position below the floor must not refuse as `unavailable`. `entry` must still answer `archived`, and the other reads must answer as they do for a head past that position. Add a native case with a real floor, covering `followReceipt` and a card refresh. If the builder believes a different answer is right, the question goes to the planner.\n\n2. A waiting projection blocks other readers of the scope.\n   - `ApplicationProjections.read` waits inside `config.flights.run` (`projection.ts:35-42`). `PreparationFlights` has one slot and answers null while it is busy (`preparation-flight.ts:14`). `ItemQueries` shares that flight (`object.ts:325-327`).\n   - Scenario: a session holder sends a projection read with a future `atLeast`, about once a second. Every other reader's `query-items` and `application-projection` read then gets an immediate `unavailable`. The wait spends no `ItemQueryBudget`, because `behind` returns before `#run`.\n   - The ordinary case has the same effect: a card refreshing after its own act holds the slot until the seal arrives, and other readers are refused meanwhile.\n   - Fix: wait before entering the flight, and add a case with a concurrent item query during a projection wait.\n\n3. A causal signed reader can miss the entry it waited for.\n   - `#rooted` runs `#chains.update()` before the wait (`object.ts:913`, `:943-944`), and nothing updates the chains after it. `#mine` then finds no root for an entry sealed during the wait (`reads.ts:146`).\n   - Scenario: a signed history read with `atLeast` N, where entry N seals during the wait and chains to the reader's key. The page omits N, with `at` at or past N. An `entry` read for a target that arrives during the wait gets `forbidden` at wake (`reads.ts:213`). A fresh read of the same position succeeds.\n   - This fails closed for authority, but it breaks what the barrier promises: \"at least this head\" for a reader who may read it.\n   - Fix: update the chains after a wake, before the check and the read. Add a native case with a causal signed reader whose entry seals during the wait.\n\n4. Two public refusals change, and the docs still promise them. This is also why the follow-up fails.\n   - `followReceipt` over a real transport now returns `unavailable` for a receipt with another hash or another kind. Main returned `hash-mismatch` and `reference-mismatch`. `packages/client/README.md:181-186` and the comment at `handle.ts:34-39` still give the old answers, and they say that each one means \"the receipt is not of this history\". `unavailable` does not say that, and a caller can read it as a passing failure.\n   - `hosted.ts:551` used to reach its 409 `enrol`/`entry` refusal on a hash mismatch. It now returns 503 `unresolved`, so a permanent condition reads as retryable.\n   - The single-file follow-up in `docs/testing.md` asks the repair to keep each case's \"assertions about production behavior\" and not to weaken them. Its other conditions hold: one file changes, nothing imports it, all stages ran, and the compiler and whole file pass.\n   - Commit ce3aadfc rewrites two such assertions to match a change in production that this head makes. Gate 3 did not fail on a defect in the test. It reported a change in a public refusal, from a specific answer to a generic one. Gate 3 stays failed, and the head has no passing gate.\n   - The adoption dc7be8f4 names `unavailable` for the barrier. It does not address `followReceipt`, its documented refusals or the hosted route.\n   - Fix: keep the named refusals in `followReceipt`, or take the change to the planner. For example, `followReceipt` can pass a qualified sequence minimum and keep its own check after the read, which also fits the request's \"pass its position\". If the planner accepts `unavailable`, the README, the comment, the hosted route and the note change with it.\n\n5. Two transports drop the minimum, and nothing notices.\n   - The CLI's wrappers in `packages/cli/src/command-support.ts:216-225` take no `atLeast`, and TypeScript allows the shorter signature. The local host in `packages/cli/src/application-dev.ts:118-121` ignores the parameter.\n   - `ScopeHandle` never checks that the reply's `at` reaches the minimum. A CLI `handle.summary(receipt)` can therefore return an older state as though the barrier held, and `followReceipt` gives different refusals through the CLI and over HTTP.\n   - Fix: carry the minimum on both, or refuse it. Have the handle treat a reply below its minimum as a failure, with a case for each.\n\n6. The kernel-gate text does not name all of the additions.\n   - The section names `ReadMinimum`, `behind`, the bound and the wire parameter. It does not name the new file `packages/bytes/src/read-minimum.ts` and its exports, `isBehindRead`, the `atLeast` parameter on five `ScopeApi` reads, or the additions of `wrong-incarnation` and `behind` to `APPLICATION_PROJECTION_REFUSALS`.\n   - Fix: name them, and say in a sentence why each belongs in the kernel.\n\nThe failed rows stay as they are: gate 1 at 6c2ad821, gate 2 at 091c0781 and gate 3 at 240c963f. The follow-ups at ce3aadfc stay their own rows.\n\n## Against the request and its rulings\n\n1. The reads and the wire. Resolved apart from items 1, 2, 3 and 5.\n   - Five reads take one optional `ReadMinimum`. The parser is closed and bounded: 512 decoded bytes, a canonical round trip, exact keys. A minimum cannot name another scope.\n   - `behind` carries the authorized current head and maps to HTTP 503. Another incarnation refuses without waiting.\n2. Authority. Resolved apart from item 3.\n   - `ReadWait.until` calls the position check on every loop, including after expiry and before it compares incarnations. An unauthorized or expired reader gets `forbidden`, with no head. The native case expires a session before the timeout.\n3. The wait. Resolved apart from item 2.\n   - Registration comes before the synchronous check, so a seal before it is seen and a seal after it wakes the read. `sealed()` runs from `Turns.onSealed`, after the commit. `finally` releases the timer and the callback. The wait writes nothing.\n4. The two consumers. Resolved apart from item 1. The card refresh sends one projection request with the receipt's full minimum, with no summary barrier and no polling. The hosted enrolment read-back uses the same minimum.\n5. The control. The hash-guard control distinguishes: its baseline passes and its mutant fails, and the source is restored. It covers the guard, not the wake.\n6. Kernel rule. Resolved apart from item 6. The head names no platform definition or application vocabulary in the kernel, and `node scripts/check-kernel-boundary.mjs` checks 127 production sources with no refusals.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The worktree is clean. `git merge-tree --write-tree` onto main 5dc9765f gives the head tree 07baa6da.\n- The 45 artifacts name exactly the 45 changed paths, all at this head. I read the note first, as the request asks.\n- This verdict names `scripts/security-entry-points.test.mjs` (62a42ef7) as its first artifact, because `gs review` refuses any other. A commitment's report is the last artifact filed under its promise, and the builder filed the note (76795853) first and that test last. The next head must file the note last, so that its report is the note.\n- I ran the owning files once each, with no whole suite:\n  - `packages/bytes/test/read-minimum`, `packages/client/test/read-minimum` and `intent`: 17 of 17;\n  - native `packages/scope/test/read-minimum`, `client` and `application-projection-native`: 8 of 8;\n  - `security-entry-points.test.mjs`: 10 of 10.\n  - The builder worktree stayed clean.\n- Gate 3 receipt 3b367c39 exits 1 with complete capture, the same head and tree before and after, and no dropped bytes. Kernel, Node, Counting and Plain pass, and Native fails only the two cases of `client.test.ts`. Receipts 92a3913c and 84b25124 exit 0 at ce3aadfc. From ce3aadfc to the head, only the note and the manifest change.\n- The evidence verifies. The manifest has SHA-256 20062e6e. All 196 listed files match by size and SHA-256. The directory has 576 files and 85,415,337 bytes, its `SHA256SUMS` digest is 64725902 and reruns clean, and no file is unlisted or writable.\n- The manifest's shape differs from ruling 4e199bd8, which names `directories` entries. It cites one whole root through `root_index_sha256`. That is enough for this head, because the citations cover the root.\n- check-doc-links on an export of the head: 761 links, 0 broken.\n- check-notes from 5dc9765f: 0 warnings.\n- check-commit-messages from 5dc9765f: 5 checked, 0 flagged.\n- `git diff --check`: clean.\n\n## Points that do not block\n\n1. `ReadWait` has no cap on waiters. A valid reader can hold many one-second waits, and one signed-read admission covers the whole wait. The request adds no rate policy, so this is for the planner to weigh.\n2. Each `behind` counts as `error` and `5xx` on the route metric (`operational.ts:112`). A reader can raise that rate at will, and ordinary use after an act can raise it too.\n3. `#projectionMinimum` prepares twice, and `#run` prepares again, so one read can make three preparation calls.\n4. A valid `atLeast` on `outbox`, `log` or `retained` is silently ignored, because `worker.ts` parses it on all routes.\n5. The signature does not cover `atLeast`, so a proxy can strip the barrier. It grants nothing, and the handle check in item 5 would catch a stripped barrier.\n6. A card holds one `readReceipt`. An act on scope A followed by an act on scope B drops the barrier for A.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe shared contract owns the record and the refusal, Scope owns the ephemeral wait, and no journal state or definition changes. That split is right. The wait sits at the wrong level for projections (item 2), and two transports do not carry the contract (item 5). Changes requested, items 2 and 5.\n\n## Security\n\nThe barrier grants nothing, and authority holds at every wake. No reader learns a head it may not read. Items 1, 3 and 4 do not widen access. They change what honest readers are told, including whether a receipt belongs to this history. Item 2 lets one session holder deny reads to others on the scope. Changes requested, items 1 to 4.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe wait is 49 lines with one wake seam, and one record serves five reads. `ReadWait` gets simpler if the projection waits outside the flight. Approved, with that point.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: changes requested, items 2 and 5.\n- Security: changes requested, items 1 to 4.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's checks:\n  - These hold: the barrier's authority and wake order, the wire, the one-request card refresh on the happy path, and the evidence.\n  - These are needed: items 1 to 5, each with its case, item 6 in the docs, and one full gate at the final head.\n  - Gate 3 stays failed. The single-file exception does not apply, because the repair rewrites assertions about production behavior.\n\nMain stays fixed for the J4 witness, as the request says. A head that makes these changes can come back for a review of its diff from this head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:62a42ef731aecaa7a05062cb6cadc1382fd44471","binding":"assigned","head":"aa20f0fe0e01e380e49497a64371958e1147e574","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d1